## Controls and first outcomes

Eight new portable cases cover exact producer/predecessor agreement, CRLF,
stale/partial/duplicated/foreign bodies, a seeded 24-mutation corpus for each
current path, fresh refusal after success, mode/blob/stage/path/index changes,
deletion and nonregular entries, and unknown-path refusal before observation.
A Unix-only ninth case checks symlink refusal before the index callback.
Preservation controls retain complete old guard files, physical reader prefixes,
original assertion tokens and the independently constructed history facade.

The first three-target candidate passes eighteen cases. The expanded Windows
cohort passes thirty, as does MSRV 1.88. Strict Clippy separately refuses the
new test's duplicated module graph: importing both facades loads eleven shared
modules twice. That first source and diagnostic are retained rather than hidden
with a lint allowance. The correction removes only the new target's redundant
history-facade import and calls; the unchanged independent authority/history
targets retain their actual reader coverage. The exact history-facade construction
and all eight new control cases remain in the new target.

After this test-only wiring correction, the changed target passes eight cases
on Windows Rust 1.94, eight on Windows MSRV 1.88 and nine on GNU/Linux Rust 1.94.
All-targets check and strict all-targets/all-features Clippy pass without lint
exceptions. Formatting, doc-check and the local structural performance contract
are checked separately. The original five-target Windows/MSRV cohorts and the
later small target have separate receipts; they are not one repeated measurement.

The first GNU five-target attempt compiles all selected targets, prints nine
